1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:01,840
So melting ice

2
00:00:01,670 --> 00:00:03,680
is a chemical change, right?

3
00:00:03,706 --> 00:00:07,589
Nope. And questions like that cost people easy points.

4
00:00:07,417 --> 00:00:11,428
This is four point one. Physical versus chemical change.

5
00:00:11,349 --> 00:00:14,531
By the end, you'll call it in about two seconds.

6
00:00:14,361 --> 00:00:17,811
I'm Mira. And it all starts with one question.

7
00:00:17,924 --> 00:00:19,878
Did a new substance form?

8
00:00:19,668 --> 00:00:23,742
If no, it's a physical change. If yes, chemical.

9
00:00:23,532 --> 00:00:29,715
So, ice. Zoom all the way in and it's H two O molecules, locked in a grid.

10
00:00:29,505 --> 00:00:34,950
Warm it up, and the grid falls apart. The molecules start sliding past each other.

11
00:00:34,780 --> 00:00:37,865
But every single one is still H two O.

12
00:00:37,695 --> 00:00:40,586
Same stuff, different arrangement. Physical.

13
00:00:40,376 --> 00:00:45,643
Boiling's the same deal. Liquid water to water vapor. Still H two O.

14
00:00:45,473 --> 00:00:49,053
So any phase change? Physical. Every time.

15
00:00:48,883 --> 00:00:51,308
Mixing and separating count too.

16
00:00:51,138 --> 00:00:55,575
Stir iron filings into sand, then pull them back out with a magnet.

17
00:00:55,397 --> 00:00:59,487
The iron didn't become anything. It just went on a little trip.

18
00:00:59,472 --> 00:01:03,770
Now a gas stove. That blue flame is methane burning.

19
00:01:03,560 --> 00:01:07,606
Methane plus oxygen gives you carbon dioxide and water.

20
00:01:07,396 --> 00:01:13,384
Look at the molecules. Before, methane and oxygen. After, totally different ones.

21
00:01:13,188 --> 00:01:17,851
The bonds broke, and the atoms rearranged into new substances.

22
00:01:17,641 --> 00:01:22,321
Same atoms, new substances. That's a chemical change.

23
00:01:22,252 --> 00:01:26,838
Quick check. Iron filings and sand, separated with a magnet.

24
00:01:26,668 --> 00:01:29,775
Physical or chemical? Pause it and try.

25
00:01:32,775 --> 00:01:38,885
Physical. Nothing new formed. Iron's still iron, sand's still sand.

26
00:01:39,003 --> 00:01:42,853
Problem is, you can't see molecules in a lab.

27
00:01:42,763 --> 00:01:46,410
So you look for evidence. There are four clues.

28
00:01:46,200 --> 00:01:49,880
One. Heat or light. Like a candle burning.

29
00:01:49,851 --> 00:01:55,391
Two. A gas forms. Baking soda and vinegar fizz out carbon dioxide.

30
00:01:55,317 --> 00:01:59,697
Three. A precipitate. That's a solid showing up out of two liquids.

31
00:01:59,527 --> 00:02:05,709
Mix lead nitrate and potassium iodide, both clear, and you get a bright yellow solid.

32
00:02:05,499 --> 00:02:11,302
Four. A color change. Like gray iron slowly turning into reddish-brown rust.

33
00:02:11,092 --> 00:02:14,062
Those four are the ones the exam uses.

34
00:02:14,182 --> 00:02:19,075
Quick check. Two clear liquids, and a cloudy white solid shows up.

35
00:02:18,945 --> 00:02:21,980
Which clue is that? Pause.

36
00:02:24,980 --> 00:02:27,289
A precipitate. Clue three.

37
00:02:27,482 --> 00:02:31,231
Now, the catch. The clues can lie.

38
00:02:31,061 --> 00:02:35,557
Boiling water makes bubbles. That's a gas. Clue two, right?

39
00:02:35,440 --> 00:02:39,401
Nope. Those bubbles are just water vapor. Physical.

40
00:02:39,191 --> 00:02:42,763
Mix blue dye and yellow dye, and you get green.

41
00:02:42,619 --> 00:02:46,534
Color change. But nothing reacted. It's just a mixture.

42
00:02:46,324 --> 00:02:53,154
And an old-school light bulb gives off heat and light. The filament's still, um... just tungsten.

43
00:02:53,064 --> 00:02:56,131
So clues are evidence, not proof.

44
00:02:55,921 --> 00:03:00,540
When you're not sure, go back to the question. Did a new substance form?

45
00:03:00,370 --> 00:03:03,591
A candle does both, which is kind of rude.

46
00:03:03,421 --> 00:03:06,923
Wax melting down the side? Still wax. Physical.

47
00:03:06,699 --> 00:03:12,201
Wax burning in the flame turns into carbon dioxide and water. Chemical.

48
00:03:11,687 --> 00:03:15,661
Last one. You snap a glow stick, and it starts glowing.

49
00:03:15,731 --> 00:03:18,228
Physical or chemical? Pause.

50
00:03:21,088 --> 00:03:28,618
Chemical. The stuff inside reacts and makes new substances. The light comes from that reaction.

51
00:03:28,640 --> 00:03:32,192
On the exam, they love particle diagrams.

52
00:03:32,022 --> 00:03:34,825
Two boxes. Before and after.

53
00:03:34,615 --> 00:03:39,817
If the after box has the same molecules, just spaced out differently, physical.

54
00:03:39,593 --> 00:03:43,214
If it has molecules that weren't there before, chemical.

55
00:03:43,084 --> 00:03:47,615
Like this. Hydrogen and oxygen before, water after.

56
00:03:47,445 --> 00:03:51,433
Same atoms on both sides, but a new molecule. Chemical.

57
00:03:51,250 --> 00:03:57,100
So on a diagram, skip the clues and just read the particles. You've got this.

58
00:03:57,043 --> 00:04:02,139
So basically. New substance? Chemical. Same substance? Physical.

59
00:04:01,938 --> 00:04:08,668
And heat, light, gas, a precipitate or a color change are clues. Not proof.

60
00:04:08,868 --> 00:04:15,824
Next one's four point two, net ionic equations. Half the ions just sit there.
